select 'bbbb0001-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A graph is best described as...","options":["a picture of a function","a set of vertices joined by edges","a list of numbers in order","a table of values"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A graph G = (V, E) is a set of vertices V connected by edges E - see ''Graphs and terminology''."},{"prompt":"In any graph, the sum of all vertex degrees equals...","options":["the number of edges","twice the number of edges","the number of vertices","half the number of edges"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each edge touches two endpoints, adding 2 to the degree total - the handshake lemma."},{"prompt":"A tree on n vertices has exactly how many edges?","options":["n","n - 1","n + 1","2n"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tree is connected and acyclic, which forces exactly n - 1 edges."},{"prompt":"A path in a graph is...","options":["any set of edges","a walk that repeats no vertex","a single vertex","a cycle of length 3"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A path visits distinct vertices in sequence, each consecutive pair joined by an edge."},{"prompt":"Two vertices are in the same connected component when...","options":["they have the same degree","there is a path between them","they share a color","they are both odd degree"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Connectivity means a path exists linking the two vertices."},{"prompt":"A graph is bipartite when its vertices can be split into...","options":["three equal groups","two sets with every edge crossing between them","one set","as many sets as vertices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bipartite means two sides with no edge inside either side."},{"prompt":"The shortest path between two vertices in an unweighted graph is found most directly by...","options":["depth-first search","breadth-first search","sorting the edges","counting cycles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Breadth-first search explores by distance, so it reaches each vertex along a shortest path first."},{"prompt":"Coloring a graph means assigning colors to vertices so that...","options":["every vertex is the same color","adjacent vertices differ in color","each edge is one color","colors follow degree order"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A proper coloring gives no edge two endpoints of the same color - see ''Graph coloring''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Graphs and terminology', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Read the basic vocabulary of graphs, count edges with the handshake lemma, and tell simple graphs apart by structure. Warm-up: at a party where everyone shakes some hands, why must the total number of hands shaken come out even?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A graph G = (V, E) is a set of vertices V together with a set of edges E, where each edge joins a pair of vertices. Two vertices joined by an edge are adjacent, and the edge is incident to each of them. The degree of a vertex is the number of edges touching it. The handshake lemma says the sum of all degrees equals twice the number of edges, because every edge is counted once at each endpoint. A direct consequence: the number of odd-degree vertices is always even. A simple graph has no loops and no repeated edges. The complete graph K_n joins every pair of vertices and has C(n, 2) = n(n - 1)/2 edges."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"The complete graph K_5 has C(5, 2) = 10 edges, and each of its 5 vertices has degree 4, giving a degree sum of 5 times 4 = 20 = 2 times 10, exactly as the handshake lemma predicts. A graph with vertex degrees 3, 3, 2, 2 has degree sum 10, so it has 10 / 2 = 5 edges. You can never build a graph with degrees 3, 3, 3 alone: that is three odd-degree vertices, and the odd-degree count must be even. K_4 has C(4, 2) = 6 edges, each vertex of degree 3."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) How many edges does K_6 have? 2) If a graph has 9 edges, what is its degree sum? 3) What is the degree of each vertex in K_5? 4) Can a graph have exactly one vertex of odd degree? 5) A graph has degrees 4, 3, 2, 2, 1 - how many edges? Answers: 1) C(6, 2) = 15. 2) 2 times 9 = 18. 3) 4, since each vertex connects to the other four. 4) No - the number of odd-degree vertices is always even. 5) degree sum 12, so 6 edges."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: forgetting that each edge adds 2, not 1, to the degree total. Mistake: allowing an odd number of odd-degree vertices. Exit: (a) How many edges are in K_n? (b) The sum of degrees equals what multiple of the edge count? Answers: C(n, 2) = n(n - 1)/2; twice the edge count."}]}'::jsonb, 'Every edge adds 1 to the degree of each of its two endpoints, so it contributes 2 to the total degree - this is why the degree sum is always even.', 12),
  (3, 'Paths, cycles, and connectivity', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Distinguish walks, paths, and cycles; decide whether a graph is connected; and count its connected components. Warm-up: if you can drive from town A to town B and from B to C, can you always drive from A to C?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A walk is a sequence of vertices where each consecutive pair is joined by an edge. A path is a walk that repeats no vertex. A cycle is a closed path of length at least 3 that returns to its start and repeats no other vertex. A graph is connected when there is a path between every pair of vertices. When it is not, it breaks into connected components - the maximal pieces within which every pair is reachable. Reachability is transitive: if A reaches B and B reaches C, then A reaches C, which is exactly what makes components well defined."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A single cycle on vertices 1-2-3-4-1 is connected: any two vertices are joined by a path going one way around the ring or the other. Remove edge 1-2 and the ring becomes the path 2-3-4-1, still connected. Now take two separate triangles sharing no vertex: that graph has 6 vertices and 2 connected components, because no path crosses from one triangle to the other. A graph with 5 vertices and 0 edges has 5 components, one per isolated vertex. The path graph 1-2-3-4-5 has one component and no cycle."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Is a single edge between two vertices a cycle? 2) What is the smallest possible length of a cycle in a simple graph? 3) A graph has vertices split into three groups with no edges between groups - how many components? 4) If a connected graph on 6 vertices loses one edge, is it always disconnected? 5) Name the term for a walk that repeats no vertex. Answers: 1) No, a cycle needs length at least 3. 2) 3 (a triangle). 3) 3 components. 4) No - a cycle can lose an edge and stay connected. 5) a path."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling any closed walk a cycle, even when it retraces an edge. Mistake: assuming removing one edge always disconnects a graph. Exit: (a) What is the minimum cycle length in a simple graph? (b) What do we call the maximal connected pieces of a graph? Answers: 3; connected components."}]}'::jsonb, 'A walk may repeat vertices and edges; a path repeats nothing; a cycle is a path that returns to its start with no other repeat. Keep the three words distinct.', 13),
  (4, 'Trees and spanning trees', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognize a tree, count its edges, and build a spanning tree of a connected graph. Warm-up: a network of 8 computers is connected with no redundant loops - how many cables does it use?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A tree is a connected graph with no cycles. A tree on n vertices has exactly n - 1 edges, and between any two vertices there is exactly one path. Adding any edge to a tree creates exactly one cycle; removing any edge disconnects it. A leaf is a vertex of degree 1, and every tree with at least two vertices has at least two leaves. A spanning tree of a connected graph G is a subgraph that includes every vertex of G and is itself a tree - a minimal set of edges that keeps everything connected. A forest is a graph whose components are all trees."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A tree with 8 vertices has 8 - 1 = 7 edges, matching the 7 cables needed to link 8 computers with no loops. The complete graph K_4 has 6 edges; any spanning tree of it keeps just 4 - 1 = 3 of them, forming a tree on all 4 vertices. A path graph 1-2-3-4-5 is already a tree with 4 edges and two leaves, vertices 1 and 5. A star with one center joined to 5 outer vertices is a tree with 6 vertices, 5 edges, and 5 leaves."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) How many edges does a tree with 12 vertices have? 2) How many edges does a spanning tree of a connected graph on 20 vertices have? 3) What is the fewest number of leaves a tree with at least two vertices can have? 4) Adding one edge to a tree creates how many cycles? 5) Is a graph with 6 vertices and 6 edges ever a tree? Answers: 1) 11. 2) 19. 3) 2. 4) exactly one. 5) No - a tree on 6 vertices has exactly 5 edges, so 6 edges forces a cycle."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking a tree may contain a cycle. Mistake: expecting a spanning tree to include every edge rather than every vertex. Exit: (a) How many edges are in a tree on n vertices? (b) What does a spanning tree of G span? Answers: n - 1; every vertex of G."}]}'::jsonb, 'A tree is connected and acyclic. Drop either property and it is no longer a tree: a disconnected acyclic graph is a forest, and a connected graph with a cycle is not a tree.', 13),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The complete graph K_6 has how many edges?","options":["12","15","30","36"],"answer":1,"explanation":"C(6, 2) = 6 times 5 / 2 = 15 edges join every pair."},{"prompt":"In a simple graph, the shortest possible cycle has length...","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A loop or a doubled edge is not allowed, so the smallest cycle is a triangle of length 3."},{"prompt":"A tree with 15 vertices has how many edges?","options":["13","14","15","16"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tree on n vertices has n - 1 edges, so 15 - 1 = 14."},{"prompt":"The maximal connected pieces of a graph are called its...","options":["leaves","cycles","connected components","degrees"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each component is a maximal set of mutually reachable vertices."},{"prompt":"Adding one edge to a tree produces...","options":["another tree","exactly one cycle","two cycles","a disconnected graph"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The new edge plus the unique existing path between its endpoints forms exactly one cycle."},{"prompt":"A graph with 5 vertices and no edges has how many connected components?","options":["0","1","5","10"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each isolated vertex is its own component, so there are 5."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Graph coloring', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Color a graph properly, find its chromatic number in small cases, and connect coloring to real scheduling. Warm-up: five exams must be timed so that no student sits two at once - what does a color stand for here?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A proper coloring assigns each vertex a color so that no edge joins two vertices of the same color. The chromatic number, written chi(G), is the fewest colors that allow a proper coloring. A graph needs 2 colors exactly when it has no odd cycle - that is exactly when it is bipartite. The complete graph K_n needs n colors, since every vertex is adjacent to every other. Coloring models conflict: put an edge between any two tasks that clash, and a proper coloring is a conflict-free schedule where each color is a time slot."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A single even cycle 1-2-3-4-1 needs 2 colors: alternate around the ring. An odd cycle 1-2-3-1 (a triangle) needs 3, since all three vertices are mutually adjacent, and indeed chi(K_3) = 3. The complete graph K_4 needs 4 colors. For exam scheduling, draw a vertex per exam and an edge between exams sharing a student; if that conflict graph colors with 3 colors, three time slots suffice. A tree always needs just 2 colors, since it has no cycle at all."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is chi of an even cycle on 6 vertices? 2) What is chi(K_5)? 3) What is the chromatic number of any tree with at least one edge? 4) A triangle needs how many colors? 5) A graph with 2 colors sufficient contains no cycle of what kind? Answers: 1) 2. 2) 5. 3) 2. 4) 3. 5) no odd cycle (it is bipartite)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: forcing non-adjacent vertices to differ - only adjacent ones must. Mistake: thinking every cycle needs 3 colors, when even cycles need only 2. Exit: (a) A graph is 2-colorable exactly when it has no what? (b) What is chi(K_n)? Answers: no odd cycle (equivalently, it is bipartite); n."}]}'::jsonb, 'A proper coloring constrains adjacent vertices only. Two vertices with no edge between them may freely share a color - that freedom is what keeps the chromatic number low.', 13),
  (7, 'Bipartite graphs and matching', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognize bipartite graphs, describe a matching, and reason about when everyone on one side can be paired. Warm-up: four workers each qualified for some of four jobs - when can you fill every job with a distinct qualified worker?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A graph is bipartite when its vertices split into two sets, often called L and R, so that every edge joins one vertex in L to one in R. A graph is bipartite exactly when it has no odd cycle, which is also exactly when it is 2-colorable. A matching is a set of edges no two of which share a vertex. A perfect matching pairs up every vertex. Hall''s condition gives the test for pairing all of L: a matching covering L exists exactly when every subset S of L is collectively joined to at least |S| vertices in R - no group of workers is starved of enough jobs."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"The complete bipartite graph K_{3,3} has two sides of 3, every left vertex joined to every right vertex, and 9 edges; it has a perfect matching pairing left i with right i. Take workers {a, b, c} and jobs {1, 2, 3} where a can do {1, 2}, b can do {2, 3}, c can do {1, 3}: a match is a-1, b-2, c-3, so all jobs fill. Now suppose a and b are each only qualified for job 1: the subset {a, b} reaches just one job, so Hall''s condition fails at |S| = 2 versus 1 neighbor, and no full matching exists. An even cycle of length 6 is bipartite; a triangle is not."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Is a triangle bipartite? 2) How many edges does K_{2,4} have? 3) A matching is a set of edges with what property? 4) By Hall''s condition, if some 3 left vertices together reach only 2 right vertices, can L be fully matched? 5) A graph is bipartite exactly when it lacks what kind of cycle? Answers: 1) No, it has an odd cycle. 2) 2 times 4 = 8. 3) no two edges share a vertex. 4) No - 3 vertices need at least 3 neighbors. 5) an odd cycle."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling a graph bipartite despite an odd cycle. Mistake: letting two matched edges share an endpoint. Exit: (a) Hall''s condition compares |S| to what quantity? (b) Bipartite is equivalent to how many colors sufficing? Answers: the number of vertices jointly adjacent to S; 2 colors."}]}'::jsonb, 'In a bipartite graph every edge crosses between the two sides; a matching then picks a set of these edges so that no two of them touch the same vertex.', 14),
  (8, 'Euler and Hamiltonian paths', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Tell an Euler trail from a Hamiltonian path and apply the degree test for Euler trails. Warm-up: can you trace a figure that uses every line exactly once without lifting your pen?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An Euler trail uses every edge of a graph exactly once; an Euler circuit is an Euler trail that ends where it began. A connected graph has an Euler circuit exactly when every vertex has even degree, and it has an Euler trail (open) exactly when exactly two vertices have odd degree - those two become the start and end. A Hamiltonian path visits every vertex exactly once, and a Hamiltonian cycle returns to the start. The Euler test is a quick degree count; deciding whether a Hamiltonian path exists is far harder in general, with no simple degree rule."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A square 1-2-3-4-1 has every vertex of degree 2, all even, so it has an Euler circuit: trace 1-2-3-4-1. The classic Konigsberg bridges reduce to a graph with four odd-degree vertices, more than two, so no Euler trail exists - the walk everyone wanted is impossible. A path graph 1-2-3 has two odd-degree endpoints (degree 1 each) and one even middle, so it has an open Euler trail from 1 to 3. For Hamiltonian, the same square 1-2-3-4-1 has a Hamiltonian cycle visiting all four vertices once and returning, but a graph can easily have an Euler circuit and no Hamiltonian cycle, or the reverse."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A connected graph has an Euler circuit when every vertex has what kind of degree? 2) How many odd-degree vertices does an open Euler trail require? 3) Does a triangle 1-2-3-1 have an Euler circuit? 4) A Hamiltonian path visits every what exactly once? 5) Does a graph with four odd-degree vertices have any Euler trail? Answers: 1) even. 2) exactly two. 3) Yes, all degrees are 2. 4) every vertex. 5) No - an Euler trail allows zero or two odd-degree vertices, not four."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: confusing edges (Euler) with vertices (Hamiltonian). Mistake: expecting a simple degree rule to settle Hamiltonian existence. Exit: (a) Euler trail is about visiting every what once? (b) How many odd-degree vertices does an Euler circuit allow? Answers: every edge; zero."}]}'::jsonb, 'An Euler trail is about using every edge exactly once; a Hamiltonian path is about visiting every vertex exactly once. Do not swap edges and vertices between the two.', 14),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The chromatic number of the complete graph K_4 is...","options":["2","3","4","5"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Every vertex is adjacent to every other, so all 4 need distinct colors."},{"prompt":"A graph is 2-colorable exactly when it has no...","options":["even cycle","odd cycle","leaf","isolated vertex"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No odd cycle is the same as bipartite, which is the same as 2-colorable."},{"prompt":"A matching in a graph is a set of edges such that...","options":["every vertex is covered","no two edges share a vertex","all edges share one vertex","edges form a cycle"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Matched edges are pairwise vertex-disjoint."},{"prompt":"A connected graph has an Euler circuit exactly when...","options":["every vertex has even degree","exactly two vertices are odd","it is a tree","it has a Hamiltonian cycle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"All-even degrees let the trail close back to its start using every edge once."},{"prompt":"The complete bipartite graph K_{3,3} has how many edges?","options":["6","9","12","18"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each of 3 left vertices joins each of 3 right vertices: 3 times 3 = 9."},{"prompt":"A Hamiltonian path is a walk that visits every...","options":["edge exactly once","vertex exactly once","cycle once","color once"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hamiltonian is about vertices; Euler is about edges."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Planar graphs', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Decide in small cases whether a graph is planar, apply Euler''s formula, and name the two forbidden graphs. Warm-up: can three houses each be connected to three utilities with no pipe crossing another?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A graph is planar if it can be drawn in the plane with no edges crossing. Such a drawing splits the plane into regions called faces, including the unbounded outer face. Euler''s formula ties them together: for a connected planar graph, V - E + F = 2, where V, E, F count vertices, edges, and faces. A consequence is that a simple planar graph on V vertices with V at least 3 has at most 3V - 6 edges. Two graphs are never planar: K_5, the complete graph on 5 vertices, and K_{3,3}, the three-houses-three-utilities graph. In fact a graph is planar exactly when it contains neither of these hidden inside it."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A cube drawn flat has V = 8, E = 12, and by Euler F = 2 - V + E = 2 - 8 + 12 = 6 faces, matching the cube''s 6 sides. Check the edge bound on K_5: it has V = 5 and E = 10, but 3V - 6 = 9, and 10 exceeds 9, so K_5 cannot be planar. The three-houses puzzle is K_{3,3}, also non-planar, so the crossing-free wiring is impossible. A tree is always planar: with E = V - 1 it draws flat with a single outer face, and Euler gives F = 2 - V + (V - 1) = 1."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) State Euler''s formula for a connected planar graph. 2) A connected planar drawing has V = 6 and E = 9 - how many faces? 3) What is the maximum edge count of a simple planar graph on 10 vertices? 4) Name the two graphs that are never planar. 5) Is K_4 planar? Answers: 1) V - E + F = 2. 2) F = 2 - 6 + 9 = 5. 3) 3 times 10 - 6 = 24. 4) K_5 and K_{3,3}. 5) Yes, K_4 can be drawn with no crossings."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: declaring a graph non-planar just because one drawing has crossings. Mistake: forgetting to count the outer region as a face. Exit: (a) What does V - E + F equal for a connected planar graph? (b) Which complete graph on 5 vertices is non-planar? Answers: 2; K_5."}]}'::jsonb, 'Planarity is about whether a graph can be drawn without crossings, not about any one drawing. A messy picture with crossings may still be planar if a clean redraw exists.', 13),
  (11, 'Shortest paths: Dijkstra intuition', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how breadth-first search and Dijkstra''s method find shortest paths, and hand-trace a small weighted example. Warm-up: driving with different road times between towns, how would you grow a set of towns whose fastest route you already know for sure?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In an unweighted graph, breadth-first search finds shortest paths by exploring outward one layer at a time, so each vertex is first reached along a path of fewest edges. When edges carry non-negative weights, Dijkstra''s method generalizes this. It keeps a tentative distance to every vertex, starting at 0 for the source and infinity elsewhere. Repeatedly it settles the unsettled vertex with the smallest tentative distance - that value is now final - and relaxes each of its edges, meaning it checks whether going through this vertex offers a cheaper route to a neighbor and lowers that neighbor''s tentative distance if so. Settling nearest-first is safe precisely because no non-negative edge can make an already-shorter route longer."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Take source A with edges A-B weight 1, A-C weight 4, B-C weight 2, C-D weight 1. Start: A = 0, others infinity. Settle A, relax to get B = 1 and C = 4. Settle B (smallest at 1), relax B-C: 1 + 2 = 3 beats 4, so C drops to 3. Settle C (now 3), relax C-D: 3 + 1 = 4, so D = 4. Settle D. Final distances: A 0, B 1, C 3, D 4, and the shortest A-to-D route is A-B-C-D. In an unweighted grid, the same answers come straight from breadth-first search counting edge layers."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What edge weights does Dijkstra''s method require? 2) In an unweighted graph, which search finds shortest paths? 3) What does ''relaxing'' an edge mean? 4) In the worked example, what is the final distance to C? 5) Once a vertex is settled in Dijkstra, can its distance change later? Answers: 1) non-negative weights. 2) breadth-first search. 3) checking whether routing through the current vertex gives a shorter distance to a neighbor, and updating if so. 4) 3. 5) No - a settled distance is final."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: running Dijkstra with negative edges and trusting the result. Mistake: settling the farthest vertex instead of the nearest. Exit: (a) Which vertex does Dijkstra settle next? (b) What search handles the unweighted case? Answers: the unsettled vertex with the smallest tentative distance; breadth-first search."}]}'::jsonb, 'Dijkstra''s method requires non-negative edge weights. With a negative weight the greedy ''settle the nearest first'' logic can lock in a wrong distance, so a different method is needed.', 14),
  (12, 'Network flow basics', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe a flow network, state the conservation and capacity rules, and connect maximum flow to minimum cut. Warm-up: water moves through pipes of different widths from a source to a sink - what fixes the greatest total that can arrive?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A flow network is a directed graph with a source s, a sink t, and a non-negative capacity on each edge. A flow assigns each edge an amount within [0, capacity], and at every vertex other than s and t the total flow in equals the total flow out - conservation. The value of a flow is the net amount leaving s, which equals the net amount arriving at t. A cut splits the vertices into a side with s and a side with t; its capacity is the total capacity of edges crossing from the s-side to the t-side. The max-flow min-cut theorem says the largest possible flow value equals the smallest possible cut capacity: the tightest bottleneck sets the ceiling."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Consider s to a with capacity 3, s to b with capacity 2, a to t with capacity 2, b to t with capacity 3, and a to b with capacity 1. One valid flow sends 2 along s-a-t and 2 along s-b-t, for value 4; conservation holds at a and b. Can we do better? The cut putting {s} on one side has crossing edges s-a and s-b with total capacity 3 + 2 = 5, while the cut with {t} alone has incoming a-t and b-t totaling 2 + 3 = 5. A tighter cut separating {s, a} from {b, t} crosses a-t, a-b, and s-b: 2 + 1 + 2 = 5. The minimum cut here is 4, matching the max flow of 4 achievable by routing 1 extra unit through a-b."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What two quantities must balance at every vertex except s and t? 2) An edge with capacity 5 can carry at most how much flow? 3) The value of a flow is measured as the net amount leaving which vertex? 4) The maximum flow equals the capacity of which cut? 5) If the tightest cut has capacity 7, what is the maximum flow? Answers: 1) flow in and flow out. 2) 5. 3) the source s. 4) the minimum cut. 5) 7."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: letting flow on an edge exceed its capacity. Mistake: forgetting conservation at interior vertices. Exit: (a) What does the max-flow min-cut theorem equate? (b) At an interior vertex, flow in equals what? Answers: maximum flow value and minimum cut capacity; flow out."}]}'::jsonb, 'Capacity limits how much can pass through an edge; flow is how much actually does. Flow on an edge can never exceed that edge''s capacity, and at every middle vertex flow in equals flow out.', 14),
  (13, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Euler''s formula for a connected planar graph states...","options":["V + E + F = 2","V - E + F = 2","V - E - F = 0","E = V - 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vertices minus edges plus faces equals 2, counting the outer face."},{"prompt":"Dijkstra''s shortest-path method requires edge weights that are...","options":["all equal","non-negative","integers only","strictly negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Non-negative weights make settling the nearest vertex first correct."},{"prompt":"In a flow network, at every vertex other than the source and sink...","options":["flow in exceeds flow out","flow in equals flow out","capacity equals flow","flow is zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Conservation forces flow in to equal flow out at interior vertices."},{"prompt":"The maximum flow from source to sink equals the...","options":["number of edges","minimum cut capacity","total capacity","number of vertices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"That is the max-flow min-cut theorem: the tightest bottleneck sets the ceiling."},{"prompt":"A simple planar graph on 10 vertices has at most how many edges?","options":["18","24","30","45"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bound 3V - 6 gives 3 times 10 - 6 = 24."},{"prompt":"In an unweighted graph, shortest paths from one vertex are found by...","options":["depth-first search","breadth-first search","sorting vertices","Euler''s formula"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Breadth-first search explores by distance layers, reaching each vertex along a fewest-edge path."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (14, 'Capstone: model a real problem as a graph', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Take a concrete scheduling problem, translate it into a graph, solve it with the tools from this course, and read the answer back into the real world. Warm-up: a university must timetable exams so no student sits two at once - which graph concept from earlier fits this exactly?"},{"heading":"The setup","text":"Six exams must be scheduled: Algebra, Biology, Chemistry, Drama, Economics, and French. Some pairs share students and so cannot run at the same time. The conflicting pairs are: Algebra-Biology, Algebra-Chemistry, Biology-Chemistry, Chemistry-Drama, Drama-Economics, Economics-French, and French-Drama. The goal is to assign each exam a time slot, using as few slots as possible, so that conflicting exams never share a slot."},{"heading":"Building the model","text":"Make one vertex per exam and one edge per conflicting pair: the vertices are A, B, C, D, E, F and the edges are A-B, A-C, B-C, C-D, D-E, E-F, F-D. A time slot is a color, and ''no two conflicting exams at once'' is exactly the rule that adjacent vertices get different colors. So the fewest slots needed is the chromatic number of this conflict graph. Notice A, B, C form a triangle, and D, E, F form another triangle, so each triangle alone already forces 3 colors - the answer cannot be smaller than 3."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Work these before reading the solution. 1) How many vertices and edges does the conflict graph have? 2) Why must the number of slots be at least 3? 3) Give a 3-slot schedule, listing which exams share each slot. 4) Which earlier idea told us the lower bound of 3? 5) If instead the exams formed a single even cycle with no triangle, how many slots would suffice? Answers: 1) 6 vertices, 7 edges. 2) The subset A, B, C is a triangle (all mutually conflicting), and a triangle needs 3 colors, so at least 3 slots. 3) Slot 1: A and D; Slot 2: B and E; Slot 3: C and F - check every conflict edge joins two different slots. 4) graph coloring, where the chromatic number of a triangle is 3. 5) 2 slots, since an even cycle is bipartite and 2-colorable."},{"heading":"Reading it back & wrap-up","text":"The coloring A,D in slot 1, B,E in slot 2, C,F in slot 3 means the registrar can run all six exams in just three sittings with no student double-booked. That is the whole arc of this course in one problem: objects became vertices, conflicts became edges, a scheduling goal became a chromatic number, and a bound came from spotting a triangle. Exit: (a) What graph quantity is the minimum number of conflict-free time slots? (b) What structure in the graph proved at least 3 slots were needed? Answers: the chromatic number; a triangle (a 3-vertex complete subgraph)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Modeling is a two-way translation: first turn the real objects into vertices and their relationships into edges, then read the graph answer back as a real-world decision.', 16),
  (15, 'Final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The sum of all vertex degrees in a graph with 11 edges is...","options":["11","22","121","5.5"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The degree sum is twice the edge count: 2 times 11 = 22."},{"prompt":"A spanning tree of a connected graph on 30 vertices has how many edges?","options":["28","29","30","31"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tree on n vertices has n - 1 edges, so 30 - 1 = 29."},{"prompt":"A graph is bipartite exactly when it contains no...","options":["even cycle","odd cycle","leaf","spanning tree"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No odd cycle is equivalent to 2-colorable, which is equivalent to bipartite."},{"prompt":"An open Euler trail exists in a connected graph exactly when the number of odd-degree vertices is...","options":["0","exactly 2","exactly 3","at least 4"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Two odd-degree vertices serve as the trail''s start and end; all others must be even."},{"prompt":"For a connected planar graph with 7 vertices and 10 edges, the number of faces is...","options":["3","5","7","17"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Euler''s formula: F = 2 - V + E = 2 - 7 + 10 = 5."},{"prompt":"Dijkstra''s method settles vertices in order of...","options":["largest tentative distance first","smallest tentative distance first","highest degree first","alphabetical order"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It finalizes the nearest unsettled vertex each step, which is safe with non-negative weights."},{"prompt":"The max-flow min-cut theorem says the maximum flow equals the...","options":["number of source edges","minimum cut capacity","total network capacity","number of vertices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The smallest cut capacity is the exact bottleneck limiting the flow."},{"prompt":"The fewest time slots needed to schedule tasks whose conflicts form a graph equals that graph''s...","options":["number of edges","chromatic number","number of faces","maximum degree"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each slot is a color, and a conflict-free schedule is a proper coloring using chi(G) colors."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0001-0000-4000-8000-000000000014' and l.position = v.pos
);
